│ adulthood ranged from 8 percent for 1- or 2-year-olds without obese parents to 79 percent for 10-to-14-year-olds     │
│ with at least one obese parent. After adjustment for parental obesity, the odds ratios for obesity in adulthood      │
│ associated with childhood obesity ranged from 1.3 (95 percent confidence interval, 0.6 to 3.0) for obesity at 1 or 2 │
│ years of age to 17.5 (7.7 to 39.5) for obesity at 15 to 17 years of age. After adjustment for the child\'s obesity   │
│ status, the odds ratios for obesity in adulthood associated with having one obese parent ranged from 2.2 (95 percent │
│ confidence interval, 1.1 to 4.3) at 15 to 17 years of age to 3.2 (1.8 to 5.7) at 1 or 2 years of age. CONCLUSIONS    │
│ Obese children under three years of age without obese parents are at low risk for obesity in adulthood, but among    │
│ older children, obesity is an increasingly important predictor of adult obesity, regardless of whether the parents   │
│ are obese. Parental obesity more than doubles the risk of adult obesity among both obese and nonobese children under │
│ 10 years of age."}]}'}.                                                                                              │
