  For each of the surveys, centile curves were drawn that at age 18 years passed through the widely used cut off        
  points of 25 and 30 kg/m(2) for adult overweight and obesity. The resulting curves were averaged to provide age and   
  sex specific cut off points from 2-18 years. CONCLUSIONS The proposed cut off points, which are less arbitrary and    
  more internationally based than current alternatives, should help to provide internationally comparable prevalence    
  rates of overweight and obesity in children. This study aimed to develop an internationally acceptable definition of  
  child overweight and obesity, specifying the measurement, reference population, and age and sex specific cut off      
